+# Cover archival
+
+Each cover ID is a ten-digit decimal number. The first four digits identify a
+million-cover item, the next two identify a 10,000-cover batch, and the last
+four identify the member within that batch. For example, cover `8819999` is
+`0008819999`, in item `covers_0008`, batch `81`. Size variants use separate
+items named `s_covers_0008`, `m_covers_0008`, and `l_covers_0008`.
+
+New batches are stored as uncompressed zip files at
+`items/<size_prefix>covers_<item_id>/<size_prefix>covers_<item_id>_<batch_id>.zip`
+under `config.data_root`. The prefix is empty for original images or `s_`,
+`m_`, or `l_` for a size variant. Members are `0008819999.jpg`,
+`0008819999-S.jpg`, `0008819999-M.jpg`, or `0008819999-L.jpg`. After upload,
+the original image URL is
+`https://archive.org/download/covers_0008/covers_0008_81.zip/0008819999.jpg`.
+
+Run `archive.archive(test=False)` to stage one query window of unarchived covers.
+It writes zip files and updates the database to local zip member paths with
+`archived=true, uploaded=false`. Missing source files are marked `failed=true`.
+The staging step holds a per-batch file lock, so concurrent workers cannot
+write the same zip at once. Re-running it skips already archived records and
+zip members already present.
+
+After staging, run `Batch(8, 81).process_pending(upload=True, finalize=True)`
+for that batch. The uploader verifies all four zip files in their respective
+Internet Archive items before finalization. Finalization sets `uploaded=true`
+and writes direct download URLs to all four database filename fields, then
+removes the staged zip files. An upload or verification failure leaves the
+batch staged for retry. `process_pending(finalize=True)` can reconcile a
+previously uploaded batch without uploading again. `test=True` prevents upload
+and finalization database changes.
+
+Legacy tar records remain readable: local tar data is served directly and a
+missing local tar redirects to its archive.org item using the stored tar name.
+The former hard-coded 8M cover range is no longer needed.
